window.__ch("<h1 id=\"part-2-product-thinking-first-principles-reasoning\">PART 2: PRODUCT THINKING &amp; FIRST-PRINCIPLES REASONING</h1>\n<h2 id=\"2-0-what-product-thinking-actually-is\">2.0 What product thinking actually is</h2>\n<p>Product thinking is the discipline of looking at any product, problem, or decision and being able to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>See it from the user's perspective, not the builder's.</li>\n<li>Reason from first principles rather than from precedent.</li>\n<li>Decompose complex problems into structured pieces.</li>\n<li>Hold multiple competing objectives in mind simultaneously without collapsing to one.</li>\n<li>Form a point of view, defend it, and update it when evidence demands.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>That last one is the hardest. Forming a point of view is risky — you might be wrong. Most people in most companies avoid forming strong points of view because being wrong is socially costly. PMs who avoid forming points of view become useful adjacents — the person who runs the meeting, takes notes, sends the update — but never the person who sets direction.</p>\n<p>Direction-setting requires conviction. Conviction without rigor is dangerous. Rigor without conviction is empty. Product thinking is the muscle that lets you have both.</p>\n<p>This chapter teaches the muscle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-1-first-principles-reasoning-what-it-actually-means\">2.1 First-principles reasoning — what it actually means</h2>\n<p>The phrase &quot;first-principles thinking&quot; has been worn smooth by overuse. Let me restore its meaning.</p>\n<p>To think from first principles means: <strong>break a problem down to the most fundamental, irreducible facts and constraints, then reason upward from there, ignoring what's currently being done.</strong></p>\n<p>The opposite is reasoning by analogy: &quot;we should do X because company Y does X&quot; or &quot;we should do X because we did X last quarter.&quot;</p>\n<p>Analogy is fast but produces conventional wisdom. First principles is slow but produces unconventional answers — the kind that win.</p>\n<h3 id=\"elon-musk-s-classic-example-dissected\">Elon Musk's classic example, dissected</h3>\n<p>Musk's most-cited first-principles example: when he was starting SpaceX, the conventional wisdom was that rockets cost ~$65M to launch. By analogy, you'd accept that and try to negotiate a 20% discount. By first principles, Musk asked: what is a rocket <em>physically</em> made of? Aluminum, titanium, copper, carbon fiber. What do those raw materials cost on commodity markets? Roughly 2% of the rocket's selling price.</p>\n<p>The other 98% was manufacturing, supply chain, and margin. None of those were physical laws. They were <em>choices</em> by existing manufacturers. SpaceX rebuilt the supply chain and reduced launch cost by an order of magnitude.</p>\n<p>The lesson for PMs is not that you should literally compute material costs. It's that <strong>the way things are done is rarely the way they have to be done.</strong> Existing solutions encode existing assumptions, and those assumptions can be wrong, outdated, or specific to a context that doesn't apply to you.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-pm-specific-first-principles-example\">A PM-specific first-principles example</h3>\n<p>Imagine you're working on the onboarding flow for a B2B SaaS product. Conversion from signup to first activation is 18%. Your manager says: &quot;Let's run A/B tests on the onboarding screens — change the copy, simplify the form, add a video.&quot;</p>\n<p>That's reasoning by analogy. Other companies improved onboarding with A/B tests; let's do that.</p>\n<p>First-principles approach: ask why does the user need an onboarding flow at all? Because they don't yet know how to get value from the product. Why don't they know? Because the product is complex and value is buried under setup. Why is value buried under setup? Because we ask them to configure 12 things before they can do anything useful.</p>\n<p>First-principles answer: the problem isn't the onboarding screens. It's that the product requires too much setup before value. The fix is not better onboarding — it's reducing the setup, by either (a) defaulting most fields, (b) making the product useful with zero setup, or (c) showing users a working sample before asking for any input.</p>\n<p>The A/B test on the screens might lift activation from 18% to 22%. Reducing setup might lift it from 18% to 60%.</p>\n<p>This is the leverage of first-principles thinking. It's not a slogan. It's a discipline. And it requires more time and more discomfort than reasoning by analogy.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-to-use-first-principles-when-to-use-analogy\">When to use first principles, when to use analogy</h3>\n<p>Both have a place.</p>\n<p>Use <strong>analogy</strong> when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Speed matters more than optimality.</li>\n<li>The problem is well-understood and others have solved it.</li>\n<li>The cost of a mediocre answer is low.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use <strong>first principles</strong> when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The problem is novel, important, or both.</li>\n<li>Existing solutions seem unsatisfying.</li>\n<li>You suspect conventional wisdom is wrong.</li>\n<li>The cost of a mediocre answer is high.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Most PMs over-use analogy and under-use first principles. Most PMs who try to use first principles all the time become slow, contrarian, and exhausting. Calibrate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-2-mece-thinking-and-issue-trees\">2.2 MECE thinking and issue trees</h2>\n<p>MECE — Mutually Exclusive, Collectively Exhaustive — is the McKinsey-pioneered way to decompose problems. Every PM should be fluent in it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-principle\">The principle</h3>\n<p>Take any problem. Break it into sub-problems. The sub-problems should be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mutually Exclusive:</strong> no overlap. A user is either active or churned, not both.</li>\n<li><strong>Collectively Exhaustive:</strong> the sub-problems cover the whole. Active + churned should cover all users.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bad MECE: &quot;Users are either power users, occasional users, or new users.&quot; (Not exhaustive — what about churned users? Not mutually exclusive — a new user could also be a power user.)</p>\n<p>Good MECE: &quot;Users are either active in the last 30 days or inactive in the last 30 days.&quot; Active users break down into &quot;power&quot; (top 10% by usage), &quot;regular&quot; (middle 80%), &quot;light&quot; (bottom 10%). Inactive users break down into &quot;never activated&quot; and &quot;activated then churned.&quot;</p>\n<p>Both axes — active/inactive, then by intensity or by activation history — are MECE. Together they form a 2×N grid that covers every user uniquely once.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mece-breakdowns\">Common MECE breakdowns</h3>\n<p>A few that recur often enough to memorize:</p>\n<p><strong>For metric drops:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>New users vs returning users.</li>\n<li>By acquisition channel.</li>\n<li>By geography.</li>\n<li>By platform (iOS vs Android vs Web).</li>\n<li>By time of day / day of week.</li>\n<li>By cohort.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For market analysis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Geography (regions / countries).</li>\n<li>Customer segment (SMB / mid-market / enterprise).</li>\n<li>Use case (job-to-be-done categories).</li>\n<li>Channel (direct / partner / marketplace).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For prioritization:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Impact / Effort 2×2.</li>\n<li>Reach × Impact × Confidence × Effort (RICE).</li>\n<li>Now / Next / Later.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For root cause analysis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>People / Process / Technology / External (the classic four).</li>\n<li>Demand-side vs supply-side (for marketplaces).</li>\n<li>Acquisition / Activation / Retention / Revenue / Referral (AARRR funnel).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You don't have to invent MECE breakdowns from scratch every time. Memorize a small set; reach for the right one based on the problem shape.</p>\n<h3 id=\"issue-trees-the-practical-tool\">Issue trees — the practical tool</h3>\n<p>An issue tree is a MECE decomposition drawn out as a tree. The root is the problem. Each level breaks the problem further. Leaves are testable hypotheses or actions.</p>\n<p>Example: &quot;Why did monthly active users drop 8% last month?&quot;</p>\n<pre><code>                  MAU dropped 8%\n                  /            \\\n                 /              \\\n        Fewer NEW users     Fewer RETURNING users\n         entering              continuing\n            /\\                     /\\\n           /  \\                   /  \\\n        New      New          Retention  Engagement\n        signups  activation   dropped    dropped\n        dropped  dropped      among      (active but\n                              activated  fewer days\n                              users      per month)\n        /\\        /\\           /\\         /\\\n       (drill   (drill        (drill    (drill\n       further  further       further   further\n       per      per           per       per\n       channel) cohort)       cohort)   feature)\n</code></pre>\n<p>At each level, you ask: which of these branches is contributing most? Then drill deeper on that branch.</p>\n<p>Issue trees are powerful because they:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Force completeness (you don't miss obvious causes).</li>\n<li>Force structure (no random brainstorming).</li>\n<li>Make your thinking visible to others (they can challenge a specific node).</li>\n<li>Convert vague problems into testable sub-questions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Build the issue-tree muscle. In an interview, every analytical question is solved with an issue tree, drawn or verbal. In a Senior PM job, every Monday morning conversation about &quot;why is X happening&quot; is an issue tree, drawn or verbal.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-3-hypothesis-driven-product-development\">2.3 Hypothesis-driven product development</h2>\n<p>The most undervalued PM skill, especially at companies with weak product cultures.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-hypothesis-structure\">The hypothesis structure</h3>\n<p>Every product decision should be expressible as a hypothesis with this shape:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>We believe that [doing X] for [user segment] will result in [outcome] because [evidence/reasoning]. We will know we're right if [measurable signal] within [timeframe].</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Each clause matters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;We believe&quot;:</strong> Acknowledges this is a bet, not a certainty.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;That doing X&quot;:</strong> Specific intervention.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;For user segment&quot;:</strong> Not &quot;users&quot; — <em>which</em> users.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Will result in outcome&quot;:</strong> Behavioral or business outcome, not feature delivery.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Because&quot;:</strong> Reasoning. The mechanism, the existing evidence, the prior work.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;We will know we're right if measurable signal&quot;:</strong> What changes in the world if you're right.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Within timeframe&quot;:</strong> Bounds the experiment.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"worked-example\">Worked example</h3>\n<p>Bad hypothesis: &quot;We should build a referral program.&quot;</p>\n<p>Good hypothesis: &quot;We believe that adding a referral program offering ₹500 credit per successful referral, targeted at users who have completed at least 3 successful transactions in the last 30 days, will increase new user signups by at least 15% over 8 weeks, because (a) our top-decile users say they want to recommend us, (b) similar referral programs at adjacent fintech apps lifted signups 12-25%, and (c) our average user's payback period of ₹400 makes ₹500 credit affordable. We will know we're right if signups attributable to referral codes exceed 15% of total signups by week 8.&quot;</p>\n<p>The good hypothesis is rigorous. It tells engineering exactly what to build. It tells the team how to measure success. It anchors honest retros if results disappoint.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-hypothesis-backlog\">The hypothesis backlog</h3>\n<p>Mature product teams maintain a hypothesis backlog — a list of hypotheses ranked by expected value of testing. The backlog is where prioritization meets discovery. We'll go deeper in Part 5.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-this-is-undervalued\">Why this is undervalued</h3>\n<p>Most companies operate on opinions, not hypotheses. The HiPPO (Highest Paid Person's Opinion) drives decisions. PMs who introduce hypothesis discipline change the conversation: instead of &quot;should we build X&quot; being a debate over preferences, it becomes &quot;let's test the hypothesis.&quot;</p>\n<p>This shifts the dynamic from politics to evidence. It's slower to install but durable once installed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-4-build-measure-learn-the-loop-properly-understood\">2.4 Build-Measure-Learn — the loop, properly understood</h2>\n<p>Eric Ries popularized Build-Measure-Learn in <em>The Lean Startup</em>. It is the central loop of modern product development.</p>\n<pre><code>            Idea\n              ↓\n            BUILD\n              ↓\n            Code/Product\n              ↓\n            MEASURE\n              ↓\n            Data\n              ↓\n            LEARN\n              ↓\n            Insight\n              ↓\n        (back to Idea)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The loop sounds obvious. Most teams break it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"where-teams-break-the-loop\">Where teams break the loop</h3>\n<p><strong>Break point 1: Building without measuring.</strong>\nMost common. Team ships features. Nobody checks what happened. The build cycle runs but the measure cycle is broken. Result: you ship endlessly, learn nothing, plateau.</p>\n<p><strong>Break point 2: Measuring without learning.</strong>\nTeam has dashboards. Nobody synthesizes. Numbers go up and down; nobody draws conclusions. The measure cycle runs but doesn't feed insight. Result: data exhaust without intelligence.</p>\n<p><strong>Break point 3: Learning without building.</strong>\nTeam writes lengthy strategy memos and reviews. Nobody acts. The learn cycle runs but doesn't translate into the next build. Result: strategy theater.</p>\n<p><strong>Break point 4: Idea poverty.</strong>\nThe loop runs but every cycle starts with weak ideas. Result: incrementalism. The team optimizes a local maximum forever.</p>\n<p>A great PM unblocks all four points. They make sure ideas are strong, builds are scoped tightly, measurement is rigorous, learning is synthesized and acted on.</p>\n<h3 id=\"loop-velocity-matters-more-than-loop-quality-sometimes\">Loop velocity matters more than loop quality (sometimes)</h3>\n<p>A loop that runs in two weeks with 60% rigor beats a loop that runs in two months with 90% rigor — usually. Speed of iteration compounds; perfect iteration cycles can't.</p>\n<p>Big Tech sometimes forgets this. So do enterprise PMs. The loop slows because of process — review cycles, stakeholder approvals, launch readiness. Some of this is necessary; most of it is friction. Senior PMs reduce the friction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-5-trade-off-thinking-the-heart-of-pm-judgment\">2.5 Trade-off thinking — the heart of PM judgment</h2>\n<p>Every product decision is a trade-off. PMs who don't make trade-offs visible are not making decisions; they're making preferences and dressing them up.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-12-most-common-product-trade-off-axes\">The 12 most common product trade-off axes</h3>\n<p>Beyond the 10 I covered in v1, here are 12 worth memorizing:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Speed vs Quality.</strong> Ship now imperfect, or ship later polished?</li>\n<li><strong>Scope vs Time.</strong> Add this feature, or hit the deadline?</li>\n<li><strong>Generalization vs Specialization.</strong> Build for many use cases, or one specific one?</li>\n<li><strong>Acquisition vs Retention.</strong> Optimize for new users, or existing ones?</li>\n<li><strong>Power users vs New users.</strong> Features for the 1% who use it heavily, or the 99% who barely use it?</li>\n<li><strong>Short-term metric vs Long-term metric.</strong> Boost activation today, even if retention dips?</li>\n<li><strong>Free user vs Paying user.</strong> Improve the free tier, or the paid tier?</li>\n<li><strong>One customer vs all customers.</strong> Build the custom feature for the big enterprise, or stay platform-pure?</li>\n<li><strong>Cost vs Quality (for AI products).</strong> Use the cheaper model, or the better model?</li>\n<li><strong>Latency vs Quality.</strong> Faster but rougher, or slower but better?</li>\n<li><strong>Visible work vs Invisible work.</strong> Ship a feature anyone can see, or fix the underlying platform that nobody sees?</li>\n<li><strong>Aggressive launch vs Safe launch.</strong> Big public launch with risk, or quiet rollout for safety?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For every PM decision, name the axis or axes you're trading off on. State both sides explicitly. State which you're picking and why.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-what-would-make-this-a-bad-decision-drill\">The &quot;what would make this a bad decision?&quot; drill</h3>\n<p>Before committing to a decision, force yourself to argue against it. The structure:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>If we [proposed action], the worst case is that [bad outcome]. This would happen because [mechanism]. The signs we're heading there would be [signal]. The cost of being wrong is [magnitude].</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you cannot generate a credible bad-case scenario, you are not thinking adversarially. Force the discipline. We'll call this the <strong>pre-mortem</strong> — covered later.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-6-taste-judgment-and-product-gut-the-most-underexplained-pm-skill\">2.6 Taste, Judgment, and Product Gut — the most underexplained PM skill</h2>\n<p>This section was the weakest in v1. I'm going to rebuild it.</p>\n<p>There are two kinds of PMs in the world. The first kind operates by frameworks: they apply RICE, run discovery, write structured PRDs, and ship competent products. The second kind operates by frameworks <em>and</em> taste: they do all of the above and they also have a feel for when the framework is wrong, when the metric is misleading, when the user-stated need conceals a different real need.</p>\n<p>The second kind become senior leaders. The first kind plateau at L4-L5.</p>\n<p>Taste, judgment, and product gut are roughly the same thing seen from different angles. Let me define each precisely:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Taste</strong> is the ability to distinguish good craft from bad craft in your domain, faster than you can articulate why. A great designer has design taste; a great editor has prose taste; a great PM has product taste.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Judgment</strong> is the ability to make good decisions under uncertainty, especially when frameworks fail or conflict. It is the meta-skill that knows which framework to use, when to override the framework, when to trust gut over data.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Product gut</strong> is the colloquial name for the integrated felt-sense that combines taste, judgment, and pattern recognition. It's what a senior PM means when they say &quot;I just don't think this will work.&quot;</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"why-these-skills-are-underexplained\">Why these skills are underexplained</h3>\n<p>Three reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p><strong>They are tacit, not explicit.</strong> You can't write a SOP for taste. It's pattern recognition built from thousands of exposures over years. People who have it can't easily teach it; people who don't have it can't easily learn it from a book.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>PM literature pretends they don't exist.</strong> Books focus on frameworks because frameworks are teachable. They downplay taste because taste is uncomfortable to discuss (it sounds elitist, it sounds unfalsifiable, it sounds like &quot;I know it when I see it&quot;).</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Companies are uncomfortable rewarding them.</strong> Companies prefer rubric-based promotion criteria, which favor framework skills. People with taste often get promoted slower than people with frameworks, until they reach a level where taste suddenly becomes the only differentiator.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>But these skills are real, and the senior PMs you most respect have them. So let's talk about how to build them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"how-taste-judgment-and-product-gut-actually-develop\">How taste, judgment, and product gut actually develop</h3>\n<p>Three ingredients, all required:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Massive exposure.</strong>\nYou cannot have product taste if you haven't used many products and felt their craft up close. The PMs with the strongest taste have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used hundreds of products as power users.</li>\n<li>Used dozens of products in adversarial mode (poking at bugs, testing edges).</li>\n<li>Read about the design decisions behind those products.</li>\n<li>Worked at multiple companies and seen multiple cultures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Exposure compounds. Every additional product you use deeply, with attention, adds a data point to the unconscious pattern matcher.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Active reflection.</strong>\nExposure alone produces consumption, not taste. The reflection layer is what converts exposure into pattern. Specifically:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>After using a new product, you ask: what worked for me as a user? What didn't? What were they optimizing for? What did they trade off?</li>\n<li>After a meeting, you ask: what did the senior PM in the room see that I didn't? What pattern were they pattern-matching to?</li>\n<li>After a launch, you ask: what surprised me? What didn't? Where was my prediction wrong, and why?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Without reflection, you can use a thousand products and have no more taste than when you started.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Feedback and calibration.</strong>\nTaste develops fastest when your predictions get tested. Specifically:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Before a launch: predict what will happen. Write it down. Compare to reality.</li>\n<li>During design review: form a private opinion before others speak. See whose opinion you align with afterward.</li>\n<li>When evaluating a candidate decision: predict the outcome before deciding. Track which predictions came true.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This loop — predict, decide, observe — is the calibration mechanism. Without it, your gut develops in a vacuum and stays inaccurate. With it, your gut converges to reality over years.</p>\n<h3 id=\"concrete-drills-to-develop-product-taste\">Concrete drills to develop product taste</h3>\n<p>Pick one. Do it for 90 days. Then pick another.</p>\n<p><strong>Drill 1: The daily teardown sentence.</strong>\nEvery day, pick one product or feature you used. Write 1-2 sentences answering: who is this for, what JTBD does it serve, what's surprising about how it's designed. Over 90 days, you'll have 90 micro-teardowns. Patterns will emerge.</p>\n<p><strong>Drill 2: The prediction journal.</strong>\nWhenever a major product or feature launches (in your company or in public), write a 2-minute prediction: will this work? Will it succeed by what metric? What's the risk? Six months later, check yourself. Calibration improves dramatically.</p>\n<p><strong>Drill 3: The product diary.</strong>\nFor one week, log every interaction with a single product. What you tried. Where you got frustrated. Where you delighted. What you wished was different. The exercise of noticing trains the noticing muscle.</p>\n<p><strong>Drill 4: Adversarial product use.</strong>\nFor one product per week, use it adversarially. Try to break it. Try edge cases. Try the unhappy paths. Notice what falls apart. This builds the gut for &quot;where will users get stuck.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Drill 5: Read design specs and PRDs.</strong>\nFind publicly available PRDs and design specs. Read them. Note what's strong and weak. Many companies share these (e.g., Stripe, GitLab handbook, Notion's published design specs). The act of reading thousands of specs accumulates into pattern recognition.</p>\n<p><strong>Drill 6: The &quot;what would make this great?&quot; question.</strong>\nFor any product you use, ask: what's the ten-times-better version of this product? What's preventing that version from existing? Forcing yourself to imagine excellence trains taste for excellence.</p>\n<p><strong>Drill 7: Compare similar products.</strong>\nPick three products in the same category (e.g., Notion / Coda / Roam, or Razorpay / Stripe / Cashfree, or Swiggy / Zomato). Use each for a week. Note differences. Theorize why each made different design choices.</p>\n<p><strong>Drill 8: Study a master.</strong>\nPick one PM, designer, or founder whose taste you respect. Read everything they've written. Watch their talks. Try to understand the decisions they made and the principles behind them. Internalize. Apply.</p>\n<p><strong>Drill 9: Live in a category until you have a strong opinion.</strong>\nFor one product category (say, fintech consumer apps), use 10 products over 3 months. By the end, you should have strong opinions about who got what right. If you don't, you weren't paying attention.</p>\n<p><strong>Drill 10: Watch yourself making product decisions.</strong>\nWhen you make a product decision, narrate aloud (or write) the reasoning. Notice when you can't articulate it. Those moments are where your gut is operating without your conscious mind being able to explain it. The act of noticing tunes the gut.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-strong-product-taste-looks-like-in-practice\">What strong product taste looks like in practice</h3>\n<p>You're in a design review. The designer presents three concepts. The team likes Concept B. You feel something off about Concept B that you can't immediately articulate. You say: &quot;I'm worried about Concept B. Let me think about why.&quot;</p>\n<p>You spend 5 minutes. You realize: Concept B optimizes for the power user case but our actual usage data shows 80% of users hit this screen once and never again. Concept A handles the one-time use better. The team agrees.</p>\n<p>That's product taste. The gut said &quot;something's off&quot; before the brain knew why. The brain caught up. The decision improved.</p>\n<p>Senior PMs do this dozens of times a week, often without articulating the gut-check at all. They just... know. The &quot;knowing&quot; is taste compounded over years.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-weak-product-taste-looks-like\">What weak product taste looks like</h3>\n<p>You're in a design review. The designer presents three concepts. The team likes Concept B. You like Concept B too because the team likes it. You don't ask why. You don't disagree. You go along.</p>\n<p>Or: you privately don't like Concept B but you can't articulate why, so you stay silent. Two months later, the launch flops because of the issue you couldn't articulate.</p>\n<p>Or: you have an opinion but you defer to the most senior person in the room as a default.</p>\n<p>All three are weak taste. All three plateau a PM at mid-level forever.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-honest-path\">The honest path</h3>\n<p>Most PMs reading this are 1-3 years into PM work. Their taste is partial. Some areas are strong (probably the ones they've worked in deeply); others are weak. The path forward is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Acknowledge where your taste is weak.</li>\n<li>Pick one area to develop intentionally.</li>\n<li>Do drills 1-10 with discipline.</li>\n<li>Calibrate against people whose taste you trust.</li>\n<li>Repeat in another area next year.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In 5 years, your taste in 5 product domains will be strong. By 10 years, you'll be the senior PM others reference. There is no shortcut. There is also no excuse for not starting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-7-the-pm-intuition-development-loop\">2.7 The PM intuition development loop</h2>\n<p>Pulling 2.6 together into a single loop:</p>\n<pre><code>              EXPOSURE\n              (use products,\n               read products,\n               see decisions)\n                     ↓\n              REFLECTION\n              (what worked?\n               what didn't?\n               why?)\n                     ↓\n              PREDICTION\n              (before next decision,\n               state what you expect)\n                     ↓\n              DECISION\n              (act on your prediction)\n                     ↓\n              OBSERVATION\n              (what actually happened?)\n                     ↓\n              CALIBRATION\n              (where was I wrong?\n               why?)\n                     ↓\n              (back to EXPOSURE,\n               with updated priors)\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you run this loop for 5-10 years across many decisions in many domains, you become a Senior PM with strong taste. If you run it casually or not at all, you become a PM who's &quot;experienced&quot; in a narrow sense — knows the playbook, can execute — but isn't trusted to set direction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-8-mental-models-20-that-earn-their-place-in-your-head\">2.8 Mental models — 20 that earn their place in your head</h2>\n<p>I had 10 in v1. Here are 20. The new ones are marked NEW.</p>\n<p>These are the models I see senior PMs reach for repeatedly. Not all are PM-specific; some are general thinking tools. All earn their place.</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-inversion-carl-jacobi\">1. Inversion (Carl Jacobi)</h3>\n<p>Solve problems backward. Instead of &quot;how do we make this product successful?&quot;, ask &quot;how would we guarantee this product fails?&quot; Then avoid those things.</p>\n<p>This works because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Failure paths are often more concrete than success paths.</li>\n<li>Avoiding stupidity is often easier than achieving brilliance.</li>\n<li>Inversion exposes hidden assumptions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>PM application: before any major launch, run a pre-mortem. &quot;It's 6 months from now and the launch failed. What killed it?&quot; The team generates a list. You address the top items.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-second-order-thinking\">2. Second-Order Thinking</h3>\n<p>When you decide to do X, what do you cause? What does <em>that</em> cause?</p>\n<p>First-order: We add a referral program. → New users go up.\nSecond-order: Referred users have lower intent than organic. → Activation rate drops. → Engineers see lower numbers and lose conviction in the funnel.\nThird-order: Conviction drop changes how the team prioritizes. → They double down on retention features instead of acquisition. → Six months later, growth stalls.</p>\n<p>Most PMs stop at first-order. Senior PMs operate at second- and third-order routinely.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-goodhart-s-law\">3. Goodhart's Law</h3>\n<p>When a metric becomes a target, it ceases to be a good metric. People game it.</p>\n<p>PM application: any metric you optimize for will be gamed by your team. The question isn't whether to use metrics — you must. The question is which metrics are gameable in low-cost ways and which are gameable only in ways that genuinely create value.</p>\n<p>NSM design (Part 7) is largely about choosing metrics whose game <em>is</em> the value.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-lindy-effect\">4. Lindy Effect</h3>\n<p>Things that have lasted N years tend to last another N years. Things that have lasted 100 years are likely to last another 100. Things that just appeared are likely to disappear soon.</p>\n<p>PM application: when evaluating tools, frameworks, or patterns, weight longevity. A pattern that's worked for 30 years (like the inverted-pyramid news article) is unlikely to fail tomorrow. A pattern that emerged 6 months ago (like a specific AI agent framework) is volatile.</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-pareto-principle-80-20\">5. Pareto Principle (80/20)</h3>\n<p>Roughly 80% of effects come from roughly 20% of causes.</p>\n<p>PM application: 20% of features drive 80% of value. 20% of customers drive 80% of revenue. 20% of bugs cause 80% of complaints. Find the 20%; obsess over it; deprioritize the 80%.</p>\n<p>Most PMs spread effort uniformly. Pareto thinking forces concentration.</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-network-effects-and-switching-costs\">6. Network Effects and Switching Costs</h3>\n<p>Some products get more valuable as more people use them (network effects). Some products get harder to leave as you use them more (switching costs).</p>\n<p>PM application: when designing or evaluating a product, ask whether it has either property. If neither, the product is fundamentally undefensible — it can be copied or replaced.</p>\n<h3 id=\"7-jobs-to-be-done-christensen-ulwick\">7. Jobs to Be Done (Christensen, Ulwick)</h3>\n<p>Users don't buy products; they hire products to do jobs. Understand the job, not the product.</p>\n<p>PM application: when stuck on a feature decision, ask &quot;what job is the user hiring this for?&quot; The answer often reframes the decision.</p>\n<h3 id=\"8-sunk-cost-fallacy\">8. Sunk Cost Fallacy</h3>\n<p>Past investments shouldn't influence future decisions; only expected future value should.</p>\n<p>PM application: when killing a project that's been running for months, the team will resist because they've sunk effort. The right framing: forget what we've spent. From here on, what's the highest-value use of remaining effort? If continuing isn't the answer, kill.</p>\n<h3 id=\"9-convex-vs-concave-bets-taleb\">9. Convex vs Concave Bets (Taleb)</h3>\n<p>Some bets have unlimited upside and bounded downside (convex — like options). Some have bounded upside and unlimited downside (concave — like writing options).</p>\n<p>PM application: among 10 features you could ship, which are convex (could become massive winners; cheap if they fail)? Concentrate on those. Avoid concave bets where the upside is small and the downside is unbounded.</p>\n<h3 id=\"10-local-vs-global-optima\">10. Local vs Global Optima</h3>\n<p>Hill-climbing finds the nearest peak, not the highest peak. To reach the highest peak, you sometimes have to come down from the local one and search elsewhere.</p>\n<p>PM application: incremental improvements optimize toward local optima. To find the next big lift, you have to abandon the local hill and explore. Companies stuck in local optima are the ones whose every quarter looks like the last and growth has stalled.</p>\n<h3 id=\"11-new-hanlon-s-razor\">11. NEW — Hanlon's Razor</h3>\n<p>&quot;Never attribute to malice that which is adequately explained by stupidity (or, more charitably, oversight).&quot;</p>\n<p>PM application: when stakeholders push back, criticize, or resist, your first instinct should be that they're confused, undercommunicated, or overworked — not that they're acting in bad faith. 90% of conflicts in PM work are misalignment masquerading as malice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"12-new-cunningham-s-law\">12. NEW — Cunningham's Law</h3>\n<p>&quot;The best way to get the right answer on the internet is not to ask a question; it's to post the wrong answer.&quot;</p>\n<p>People are more motivated to correct errors than to volunteer information.</p>\n<p>PM application: when stuck, write up your draft thinking and share it. Critics will descend. Their corrections, often, are gold. This is also why &quot;release early, release often&quot; works — putting a half-finished thing in the world generates more high-quality feedback than asking for input on a blank page.</p>\n<h3 id=\"13-new-the-fundamental-attribution-error\">13. NEW — The Fundamental Attribution Error</h3>\n<p>People over-attribute behavior to character and under-attribute to context. We say &quot;John is lazy&quot; when in fact John is overworked.</p>\n<p>PM application: when an engineer pushes back hard or a designer seems uncooperative, ask: what context is shaping their behavior right now? Bad sleep? Unrealistic deadlines from above? An unresolved fight with their manager? Most of the time, behavior reflects context, not character.</p>\n<h3 id=\"14-new-survivorship-bias\">14. NEW — Survivorship Bias</h3>\n<p>We see successes; we don't see failures. We learn from companies that survived; we don't learn from the ones that died.</p>\n<p>PM application: when studying &quot;successful&quot; products, remember you're seeing a tiny biased sample. The same playbook that produced Notion produced 100 dead competitors. Don't blindly copy the survivor; understand the <em>distribution</em> of outcomes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"15-new-the-map-vs-the-territory\">15. NEW — The Map vs The Territory</h3>\n<p>The map is not the territory. The model is not the system. Your dashboard is not the user experience.</p>\n<p>PM application: dashboards lie by simplification. User research is sampled. PRDs are abstractions. None of them <em>are</em> the product. Senior PMs return frequently to the territory — to the actual user actually using the actual product — because the maps drift.</p>\n<h3 id=\"16-new-chesterton-s-fence\">16. NEW — Chesterton's Fence</h3>\n<p>If you encounter a fence in the middle of a field and don't know why it's there, don't tear it down. Find out why it was built first.</p>\n<p>PM application: existing features and processes often look stupid until you understand why they exist. Before deprecating, removing, or changing, ask: why was this here? Many &quot;obvious improvements&quot; by new PMs reverse decisions made by previous PMs for good reasons.</p>\n<p>This is also the antidote to the worst kind of PM hubris.</p>\n<h3 id=\"17-new-the-streetlight-effect\">17. NEW — The Streetlight Effect</h3>\n<p>Looking for your keys under the streetlight, not because that's where you dropped them but because that's where the light is.</p>\n<p>PM application: PMs over-optimize what's easy to measure, not what matters. Engagement metrics are easy; long-term retention is hard. NPS is easy; actual satisfaction is hard. A/B test results are easy; second-order effects are hard. Don't only solve what's easy to see.</p>\n<h3 id=\"18-new-optionality\">18. NEW — Optionality</h3>\n<p>When you can choose between (a) a fixed outcome and (b) a range of outcomes, the range is more valuable when uncertainty is high. Optionality is the value of being able to choose later.</p>\n<p>PM application: when uncertainty about the right path is high, prefer options that preserve future choice. Modular architectures, smaller commitments, reversible decisions. Avoid option-killing decisions early.</p>\n<h3 id=\"19-new-slack-in-systems\">19. NEW — Slack (in systems)</h3>\n<p>A system with no slack — no buffer, no idle capacity — cannot absorb shocks. It falls over at the first disruption.</p>\n<p>PM application: roadmaps with zero slack break at the first dependency slip. Teams with zero slack break at the first incident. Engineers with zero slack break at the first hard bug. Build slack intentionally. The PMs who pack every sprint to 100% capacity are the ones whose teams burn out and miss commitments.</p>\n<h3 id=\"20-new-the-ergodicity-problem-taleb\">20. NEW — The Ergodicity Problem (Taleb)</h3>\n<p>The average outcome of a population is not the same as the average outcome over time for an individual. A casino with positive expected value across all gamblers can still ruin every individual gambler over enough time.</p>\n<p>PM application: averages mislead. The &quot;average user retention&quot; may be 60% but if it's 90% for one cohort and 30% for another, the averages don't tell you the structure. Look at distributions, not just means. And: a strategy that's positive expected value across many bets can still ruin you on a single bet if the variance is wide enough — risk management matters even when EV is positive.</p>\n<h3 id=\"using-mental-models-in-practice\">Using mental models in practice</h3>\n<p>Don't try to apply 20 models to every decision. That's worse than applying none.</p>\n<p>The skill is knowing which 1-2 models apply to a given situation. That comes from repeated exposure. Read each model. Look for it in your work this week. Notice when it applies. Over a year, the right models will surface unconsciously when you need them.</p>\n<p>A separate skill: when a decision feels stuck, run through 5-10 models and see which one reframes the problem. The act of trying multiple lenses usually surfaces something.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-9-the-pm-thinking-templates\">2.9 The PM thinking templates</h2>\n<p>Before any major decision, run one or more of these templates. They're cheap, fast, and prevent most PM blunders.</p>\n<h3 id=\"template-1-the-decision-memo-1-page\">Template 1: The Decision Memo (1-page)</h3>\n<pre><code>DECISION: [What you're proposing]\n\nCONTEXT: [Why this is on the table now]\n\nOPTIONS CONSIDERED:\n  A: [Option] — Pros: ... Cons: ... Cost: ...\n  B: [Option] — Pros: ... Cons: ... Cost: ...\n  C: [Option] — Pros: ... Cons: ... Cost: ...\n\nRECOMMENDATION: [Which option, with one-sentence rationale]\n\nKEY ASSUMPTIONS: [What has to be true for this to work]\n\nWHAT WOULD CHANGE THE DECISION: [What evidence would flip you]\n\nRISKS AND MITIGATIONS: [Top 3 risks, how addressed]\n\nASK: [What you need from the reader — approval, feedback, etc.]\n</code></pre>\n<p>This template forces clarity. If you can't fill it in for a decision, you're not ready to make the decision.</p>\n<h3 id=\"template-2-the-problem-definition\">Template 2: The Problem Definition</h3>\n<pre><code>PROBLEM: [One-sentence problem statement, in user's voice]\n\nWHO HAS THIS PROBLEM: [Specific segment]\n\nHOW OFTEN: [Frequency — daily / weekly / per-event]\n\nHOW PAINFUL: [Intensity — annoying / costly / blocking]\n\nCURRENT WORKAROUND: [What they do today]\n\nWHAT WOULD GREAT LOOK LIKE: [The job, well-done]\n\nEVIDENCE THIS IS REAL: [User quotes, data, observations]\n</code></pre>\n<p>If your problem definition fits in this template, you're ready to start designing solutions. If it doesn't, do more discovery.</p>\n<h3 id=\"template-3-the-pre-mortem\">Template 3: The Pre-Mortem</h3>\n<pre><code>DATE: [Six months from now]\n\nOUTCOME: [The launch failed / the project was killed / the metric didn't move]\n\nWHY IT FAILED — top 5 candidate causes:\n  1. [Cause] — Likelihood: H/M/L. Mitigation: ...\n  2. [Cause] — Likelihood: H/M/L. Mitigation: ...\n  3. ...\n\nWHAT WE'RE WATCHING FOR: [Leading indicators of each failure mode]\n\nKILL CRITERIA: [Conditions under which we stop]\n</code></pre>\n<p>Run this before any major launch. The discipline of imagining failure surfaces the risks you'd otherwise miss.</p>\n<h3 id=\"template-4-the-5-why-drill\">Template 4: The 5-Why Drill</h3>\n<p>When investigating root causes, ask &quot;why?&quot; five times. Each layer goes deeper than the last.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Activation rate dropped this week. <strong>Why?</strong></li>\n<li>Fewer new users completed onboarding. <strong>Why?</strong></li>\n<li>They're dropping at step 3. <strong>Why?</strong></li>\n<li>Step 3 requires connecting their bank account. <strong>Why is that suddenly a problem?</strong></li>\n<li>We changed the UI last week. <strong>Why did the UI change cause drops?</strong></li>\n<li>The new UI moved the security explanation below the fold. Users don't see it. They get scared and abandon.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Five whys gets you from &quot;metric dropped&quot; to &quot;specific UI change caused trust failure.&quot; That's actionable.</p>\n<p>The discipline matters. Most teams stop at &quot;why&quot; 1 or 2. Senior PMs go to 5.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-10-practice-exercises-for-part-2\">2.10 Practice exercises for Part 2</h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-2-1-first-principles-drill\">Exercise 2.1 — First-principles drill</h3>\n<p>Take a current product feature or process at your company. Apply first-principles thinking. Strip it back to fundamental assumptions. Ask: would we design this the same way if we were starting over today, knowing what we know now? Write 500 words. Identify one unconventional move suggested by the exercise.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-2-2-issue-tree-practice\">Exercise 2.2 — Issue tree practice</h3>\n<p>Build an issue tree for: &quot;Our app's daily active users dropped 12% over the last month.&quot; Aim for 3 levels deep. At each level, force MECE.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-2-3-hypothesis-rewriting\">Exercise 2.3 — Hypothesis rewriting</h3>\n<p>Take three product decisions made by your team in the last quarter. Rewrite each as a structured hypothesis using the format in 2.3. If the original decision wasn't expressible as a hypothesis, that's information about the decision quality.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-2-4-the-12-trade-off-audit\">Exercise 2.4 — The 12 trade-off audit</h3>\n<p>For your current product or area, identify which of the 12 trade-off axes you're currently making. Are you making them deliberately, or by default? What would change if you reversed your current trade-off on one axis?</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-2-5-mental-model-application\">Exercise 2.5 — Mental model application</h3>\n<p>Pick one product decision you'll make this week. Run the decision through 5 mental models from the list of 20. For each, ask: does this model add insight here? Take notes on which models illuminate, which don't.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-2-6-pre-mortem-on-a-current-project\">Exercise 2.6 — Pre-mortem on a current project</h3>\n<p>For a project you're currently leading or contributing to, run the pre-mortem template. Identify the top 5 things that could kill it. For each, identify a leading indicator and a mitigation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exercise-2-7-build-the-prediction-journal\">Exercise 2.7 — Build the prediction journal</h3>\n<p>Start a prediction journal today. For the next 30 days, write a brief prediction (2-3 sentences) for any product decision you witness, vote on, or contribute to. After 30 days, review. Where were you right? Where wrong? What's your hit rate? What patterns emerge in your blind spots?</p>\n<p>This is the single most powerful taste-development drill in this book. Most readers will skip it. The ones who don't will be different PMs in two years.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-11-anti-frameworks-frameworks-that-are-wrong-but-interviewers-love\">2.11 Anti-frameworks: frameworks that are wrong but interviewers love</h2>\n<p>Some frameworks survive in PM culture not because they're rigorous but because they're memorable. You should know them, use them in interviews when needed, and not believe in them privately.</p>\n<h3 id=\"circles-lewis-lin\">CIRCLES (Lewis Lin)</h3>\n<p>Comprehend, Identify, Report, Cut, List, Evaluate, Summarize. The standard product-design interview framework.</p>\n<p>What's right about it: it imposes structure where most candidates have none. Interviewers like it because it gives them a way to score answers consistently.</p>\n<p>What's wrong about it: real PMs don't think this way. The structure is performative. Excellent answers often deviate from it because the problem doesn't fit. Slavishly following CIRCLES produces formulaic answers that competent interviewers see through.</p>\n<p>The trick: use the structure as scaffolding when you need it. Deviate when the problem demands. Show that you can use it <em>and</em> know when not to.</p>\n<h3 id=\"aarrr-pirate-metrics-acquisition-activation-retention-revenue-referral\">AARRR (Pirate Metrics — Acquisition, Activation, Retention, Revenue, Referral)</h3>\n<p>Useful as a vocabulary. Misleading as a strategic framework because it implies all products optimize across all five stages equally. Most products at most stages prioritize 1-2 of these intensely and ignore the others. Use it as a checklist, not a priority list.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-lean-startup-mvp-loop\">The Lean Startup MVP loop</h3>\n<p>Build-Measure-Learn is right in spirit, deeply oversimplified in practice. Real product development has loops within loops, with different cadences for different decisions. Don't take the loop diagram as the operating model. Take it as one cadence among many.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rice-reach-impact-confidence-effort\">RICE (Reach × Impact × Confidence ÷ Effort)</h3>\n<p>The most-used prioritization framework. It works decently for tactical features. It fails for strategic decisions where Reach is unknown, Impact is uncertain by an order of magnitude, and Effort is similar. RICE applied to strategic decisions produces false precision. Use Impact-vs-Effort 2×2s and qualitative reasoning instead.</p>\n<h3 id=\"nps-net-promoter-score\">NPS (Net Promoter Score)</h3>\n<p>Surprisingly weak as a real metric. The 0-10 scale is too coarse. The &quot;would you recommend&quot; question is too far removed from actual usage. NPS is useful as a long-running directional signal, useless as a feature-decision input. Yet companies treat it as a sacred metric. Use it as one input, not the source of truth.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-to-do-with-anti-frameworks\">What to do with anti-frameworks</h3>\n<p>In interviews: invoke the framework when it's expected. Show competence. Then pivot to your actual reasoning, which is sharper.</p>\n<p>In real work: bypass them. Use the underlying skills (decomposition, evidence-based reasoning, trade-off analysis) directly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-12-if-you-remember-only-one-thing-from-part-2\">2.12 If you remember only one thing from Part 2</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Product thinking is not knowing the frameworks; it's knowing when to apply them, when to break them, and when to think from scratch. The frameworks are scaffolding. The thinking is yours. Most PMs never make this distinction. The ones who do, become senior.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"2-13-google-lens-what-google-interviewers-test-for-in-product-thinking\">2.13 Google Lens — what Google interviewers test for in product thinking</h2>\n<p>(Throughout the book, &quot;Google Lens&quot; callouts highlight what specific PM skills look like through the eyes of a Google interviewer, based on patterns from public APM rubrics, Lenny Rachitsky's interviews with ex-Google PMs, Lewis Lin's published frameworks, and Glassdoor candidate reports. These are pattern-matched signals, not insider information.)</p>\n<p>For product thinking specifically:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Can the candidate <strong>decompose ambiguous problems</strong>? (Issue trees.)</li>\n<li>Can the candidate <strong>form a point of view and defend it under pressure</strong>? (Conviction with rigor.)</li>\n<li>Does the candidate <strong>distinguish hypothesis from belief</strong>? (Falsifiability.)</li>\n<li>Does the candidate <strong>call out their own assumptions</strong>? (Meta-awareness.)</li>\n<li>Does the candidate <strong>show signs of taste — picking up on subtle product issues</strong>? (The hardest signal to fake.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The bar at Google is not &quot;can you produce a structured answer.&quot; It's &quot;can you produce a structured answer <em>and</em> show evidence of judgment that goes beyond the structure.&quot; That second part is what taste, mental models, and the prediction journal are for.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><em>End of Part 2, v2.</em></p>\n<p>\\newpage</p>\n");
